--TEST--
`Core\Jwt::verifyIssued<T>` refuses a written type whose text fields are not `tainted`, at the call that writes it and at the class that declared it — `rule:security/derived-codec-qualifiers`, `rule:security/verification-does-not-launder`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A signature proves who wrote a claim, not that it is safe for any sink, so
// the claims come back qualified — and a codec adds no qualifier rule of its
// own, which means the fields receiving them have to say so where they are
// written. An inline shape says it in one word; a class says it once per text
// field, and is reported at the declaration that put the field on the wire
// contract rather than at every call that decodes into it.
#[Core\Json\Derive]
final class Session {
    public string $sub;

    public function constructor(string $sub) {
        $this->sub = $sub;
    }
}

Core\Crypto\KeyPair $issuer = Core\Crypto::generateKeyPair(Core\Crypto\KeyKind::Ed25519);
string $token = Core\Jwt::signObject(
    Core\Arr::shapeAs<{iss: string}>(["iss" => "https://issuer.example"]),
    15m,
    $issuer
);

var $shaped = Core\Jwt::verifyIssued<{iss: string, exp: int}>(
    $token,
    $issuer->publicKey(),
    "https://issuer.example",
    "novis-app"
);
Session $declared = Core\Jwt::verifyIssued<Session>(
    $token,
    $issuer->publicKey(),
    "https://issuer.example",
    "novis-app"
);
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0810]: `Session::$sub` is declared `string`, and `Core\Jwt::verifyIssued` answers a peer's octets
   --> case.nvs:10:19
%A
error[E0810]: `iss` is declared `string` in `{exp: int, iss: string}`, and `Core\Jwt::verifyIssued` answers a peer's octets
   --> case.nvs:24:38
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
